      "e": "In C and C++, two-dimensional arrays are stored in contiguous row-major memory. Iterating `arr[i][j]` accesses sequential memory addresses, loading an entire cache line (typically 64 bytes) that satisfies subsequent reads immediately. In contrast, column-major access jumps across rows by stride of N elements, incurring a cache miss on almost every access.",
      "step": "Step 1: Row-major layout: arr[i][j] address = Base + (i * cols + j) * sizeof(elem).\nStep 2: Stride of 1 element loads consecutive cache lines (Spatial Locality).\nStep 3: Column-major stride = 14 * sizeof(elem), jumping across memory pages and causing TLB misses.\nStep 4: Speedup can exceed 10x-20x for large N.",
      "tech": "Spatial memory locality and CPU cache architecture.",
      "formula": "\\text{Address}(i, j) = \\text{Base} + (i \\times N + j) \\times S",
